ead constitutional texts as sources of legal authority and political argument. Students practice moving from text to clause, doctrine, institution, and contemporary debate.", "children": [{"id": "ef775286-4277-471e-8386-e1d723ebfa63", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Reading Founding Documents", "children": [{"id": "8a615458-b7eb-42ca-9281-8c231e5e1e3e", "type": "concept", "title": "Declaration of Independence, Constitution, Bill of Rights, Federalist Papers, Anti-Federalist writings"}, {"id": "dd830e4f-4492-4e08-906e-aa45fbd4aefa", "type": "concept", "title": "Preamble, articles, clauses, amendments, enumerated powers, implied powers, reserved powers"}, {"id": "5616ecd4-eb0a-47fb-8bcc-d856fcffd74b", "type": "concept", "title": "Supremacy Clause, Necessary and Proper Clause, Commerce Clause, Taxing and Spending Clause, Take Care Clause"}, {"id": "990ba8c5-5ea6-41d1-b4a1-cc2234c4a811", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary-source context, audience, purpose, authorship, historical interpretation, and constitutional argument"}], "description": "Students learn how to extract claims, powers, limits, and assumptions from primary documents."}, {"id": "d10164ea-85c9-49c8-8a2b-9c87a07df410", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Methods of Constitutional Interpretation", "children": [{"id": "a5a77580-9648-403d-93ce-b37c3ab7323d", "type": "concept", "title": "Textualism, originalism, living constitutionalism, structural interpretation, doctrinal interpretation, prudential reasoning"}, {"id": "60d9baa8-3f02-47b0-a898-5f51158a83d9", "type": "concept", "title": "Precedent, stare decisis, historical practice, constitutional structure, consequences, and democratic legitimacy"}, {"id": "cfb397ee-c4f0-49a4-931d-d741d9f03342", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial restraint, judicial activism, departmentalism, popular constitutionalism, and constitutional hardball"}, {"id": "e7314447-10b2-4443-82c8-4b939e837759", "type": "concept", "title": "Ambiguity, contested meaning, interpretive communities, and constitutional change without amendment"}], "description": "Students compare ways judges, politicians, scholars, and citizens argue about constitutional meaning."}, {"id": "ac459dcd-c2dc-430d-84c0-f73a1bf79f67", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional Change and Amendment", "children": [{"id": "cbc29d22-5908-45fd-9dc2-a78f81008b8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Article V amendment process, proposal, ratification, supermajorities, state conventions, congressional proposal"}, {"id": "170aba8a-d408-4879-a31a-f336ca055eeb", "type": "concept", "title": "Bill of Rights, Reconstruction Amendments, Progressive amendments, voting-rights amendments, presidential succession"}, {"id": "e1c89e76-d203-4c87-a29d-733cc1c56fa5", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial interpretation, legislation, executive practice, party development, administrative state, social movements"}, {"id": "2320125e-995d-4487-9dbf-de398f7d69b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitutional conventions, failed amendments, reform proposals, and barriers to change"}], "description": "Students learn how formal and informal constitutional change occurs."}]}, {"id": "4d08169c-9967-487b-9463-72b595d5a4af", "type": "topic", "title": "Federalism and Intergovernmental Relations", "description": "Federalism is one of the central organizing features of American government. Students learn how divided authority creates policy variation, experimentation, conflict, and coordination problems.", "children": [{"id": "cc7406fa-b810-4d0e-afdb-dc2c03ceda3f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Division of Powers Across Governments", "children": [{"id": "b7e87d6d-7288-4cca-80df-5f7dd948ae0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Enumerated powers, implied powers, reserved powers, concurrent powers, police powers, preemption"}, {"id": "7fdcdd52-0601-4bbb-ba08-15b9f80f6579", "type": "concept", "title": "Tenth Amendment, Supremacy Clause, Commerce Clause, Necessary and Proper Clause, spending power"}, {"id": "b1d42c03-1ebd-4648-af2b-3ef143762b63", "type": "concept", "title": "National government, state governments, local governments, tribal sovereignty, territories, special districts"}, {"id": "d4d00fc7-3c51-465f-a580-23793e4f9bf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Dual federalism, cooperative federalism, coercive federalism, competitive federalism, new federalism"}], "description": "Students learn which powers belong to national, state, and local governments and why the boundaries are often contested."}, {"id": "2559d578-4b51-4642-9bf3-df5ef0e86692", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism in Policy Practice", "children": [{"id": "c61e17b5-1b6f-4ec8-913d-842d17a8bcaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Education, health care, Medicaid, transportation, elections, criminal justice, environment, disaster response"}, {"id": "876985f3-b029-4906-aa58-0046fe2dac1a", "type": "concept", "title": "Grants-in-aid, categorical grants, block grants, mandates, waivers, matching funds, maintenance of effort"}, {"id": "f5eb3619-df21-4525-adc3-382d2f05de55", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy laboratories, state experimentation, race to the bottom, spillover effects, unfunded mandates"}, {"id": "fc705f57-6cc1-420d-80e1-bca663cc878e", "type": "concept", "title": "Intergovernmental lobbying, state attorneys general, governors, legislatures, mayors, and federal agencies"}], "description": "Students examine how federalism affects real policy areas people encounter."}, {"id": "cc0d7e94-3133-435e-9496-94b9a380da77", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Federalism Conflicts and Accountability", "children": [{"id": "a843f1e9-39b2-4d6b-bf0a-be9e63ee39a4", "type": "concept", "title": "Nullification debates, civil rights enforcement, marijuana policy, immigration enforcement, pandemic response"}, {"id": "557521a9-033c-4b5b-badc-01e33f00c63e", "type": "concept", "title": "Accountability, blame shifting, venue shopping, forum shopping, state-federal lawsuits, injunctions"}, {"id": "709610ae-19f5-43ae-aecd-d8888a33bddc", "type": "concept", "title": "Preemption conflicts, commandeering doctrine, anti-commandeering, sanctuary policies, and conditional spending"}, {"id": "5254d387-1393-43f5-b46d-1276f5a177cf", "type": "concept", "title": "Equity and variation, regional inequality, local autonomy, and national standards"}], "description": "Students learn why divided authority can both protect liberty and blur responsibility."}]}, {"id": "49f2ed50-cd3d-4cc5-a2f8-f04cb13d1474", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil Liberties and the Bill of Rights", "description": "Civil liberties limit what government may do to individuals. Students study how rights claims develop through text, doctrine, cases, and conflicts over security, liberty, equality, and order.", "children": [{"id": "76ea92ad-f6eb-437e-aa24-6cadf11f1ded", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Incorporation and Due Process", "children": [{"id": "a702cff0-c0ea-4faa-80aa-792530a79dac", "type": "concept", "title": "Bill of Rights, Fourteenth Amendment, Due Process Clause, selective incorporation, fundamental rights"}, {"id": "4f85be58-1922-421b-a801-bb9eaa202218", "type": "concept", "title": "Procedural due process, substantive due process, notice, hearing, fairness, liberty interests, property interests"}, {"id": "d1e5bc71-564e-44e1-93e5-af2825dffa99", "type": "concept", "title": "Gitlow v. New York, incorporation doctrine, state action, and limits on private conduct claims"}, {"id": "7ff6b4c0-2665-4596-8da0-2706ac14ca8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Privacy, bodily autonomy, family rights, marriage, contraception, abortion debates, and constitutional change"}], "description": "Students learn how the Bill of Rights came to apply to states and why due process matters."}, {"id": "45616003-d734-4fca-b4c6-e6323827e0da", "type": "subtopic", "title": "First Amendment Liberties", "children": [{"id": "ca60c8c2-6db1-41a3-86f6-3a0af2b1a508", "type": "concept", "title": "Free speech, symbolic speech, political speech, commercial speech, obscenity, defamation, incitement, true threats"}, {"id": "f8bde7b4-fb71-4ffb-b0db-0dc1deab9c83", "type": "concept", "title": "Prior restraint, time-place-manner restrictions, public forum doctrine, strict scrutiny, content neutrality"}, {"id": "b9b6f05e-e88b-429a-9c63-e46380647df3", "type": "concept", "title": "Free Exercise Clause, Establishment Clause, religious accommodation, neutrality, coercion, and public funding"}, {"id": "f66f04db-60e5-4ba1-a8bb-d2c73828d687", "type": "concept", "title": "Freedom of the press, assembly, petition, protest rights, campaign speech, and online expression"}], "description": "Students examine expression, religion, press, assembly, and petition as foundations of democratic politics."}, {"id": "74ddd65b-898e-440d-8c50-0d02197492de", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Criminal Procedure and Security Liberties", "children": [{"id": "1d390a17-5b08-4442-8d4b-004a559ec661", "type": "concept", "title": "Fourth Amendment, search, seizure, warrant, probable cause, reasonable suspicion, exclusionary rule"}, {"id": "944396c7-80b4-44bf-b5bd-6f2da40705b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Fifth Amendment, self-incrimination, due process, grand jury, double jeopardy, Miranda rights"}, {"id": "620baad0-ce27-40d4-ab9e-81da720e431a", "type": "concept", "title": "Sixth Amendment, counsel, speedy trial, public trial, jury trial, confrontation, plea bargaining"}, {"id": "994cea68-b856-4c12-ba65-cc8dedb56e83", "type": "concept", "title": "Eighth Amendment, cruel and unusual punishment, bail, proportionality, death penalty, and prison conditions"}], "description": "Students learn how rights shape policing, prosecution, punishment, and public safety."}]}, {"id": "269a9d42-a99a-4b18-b8d0-739fe9c0289b", "type": "topic", "title": "Civil Rights, Equal Protection, and Democratic Inclusion", "description": "Civil rights concern equal treatment and meaningful inclusion in public life. Students study law, movements, institutions, and policy tools used to challenge exclusion.", "children": [{"id": "c59522bc-2700-4db3-baea-785584ebbb11", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equal Protection and Anti-Discrimination Law", "children": [{"id": "6ed3cd0e-07bd-4876-9971-90e58721a0c6", "type": "concept", "title": "Equal Protection Clause, suspect classification, fundamental rights, strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny, rational basis"}, {"id": "90b3de31-eee9-4f67-9eb4-45bc3c4bd5a1", "type": "concept", "title": "De jure discrimination, de facto discrimination, disparate treatment, disparate impact, affirmative action"}, {"id": "3d0a725b-fe78-4dab-8207-4b1fb257b60e", "type": "concept", "title": "Civil Rights Act, Voting Rights Act, Fair Housing Act, Title IX, Americans with Disabilities Act"}, {"id": "6109a26b-051c-4b54-9757-144b0a3ce268", "type": "concept", "title": "State action doctrine, intent requirement, remedies, injunctions, consent decrees, and enforcement agencies"}], "description": "Students learn the constitutional and statutory tools used to evaluate unequal treatment."}, {"id": "528f8722-9322-466b-9000-452281797e2e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Social Movements and Rights Expansion", "children": [{"id": "a7d58e26-8257-4b51-bf08-9f85a774a0eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Abolition, Reconstruction, civil rights movement, women's suffrage, labor movements, disability rights, LGBTQ+ rights"}, {"id": "c419e911-2c25-40be-b2d0-633d263378c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Litigation strategy, direct action, protest, boycotts, coalition-building, public opinion, media strategy"}, {"id": "2bd7139d-44d7-476b-bdfb-c1a2008c6b75", "type": "concept", "title": "Brown v. Board of Education, Loving v. Virginia, Obergefell v. Hodges, Shelby County v. Holder"}, {"id": "30fa3fac-2a1f-4042-ab33-be49a0024a07", "type": "concept", "title": "Backlash, countermobilization, rights retrenchment, and movement sustainability"}], "description": "Students connect legal change to organized political struggle."}, {"id": "7146472a-55e0-4e7f-b0c1-bd5d17855383", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voting Rights and Representation", "children": [{"id": "321ed7dc-c8c7-4085-83ab-745cf262539a", "type": "concept", "title": "Fifteenth, Nineteenth, Twenty-Fourth, and Twenty-Sixth Amendments"}, {"id": "c01763e4-d20c-4ba7-901d-ebcf11bf889e", "type": "concept", "title": "Poll tax, literacy test, grandfather clause, white primary, voter ID, registration rules, felon disenfranchisement"}, {"id": "3dfdc9b9-56d1-4770-87cd-dfd9035fa9bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Voting Rights Act, preclearance, Section 2, racial gerrymandering, vote dilution, language access"}, {"id": "93462170-5063-42c4-a17d-96e4b9453ac6", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive representation, substantive representation, majority-minority districts, and electoral accountability"}], "description": "Students learn why democratic inclusion depends on access to the vote and fair representation."}]}, {"id": "5a2660cb-507f-4510-b5df-e078d1e73aa6", "type": "topic", "title": "Public Opinion, Political Socialization, Ideology, and Polarization", "description": "This section teaches students how citizens develop beliefs and how those beliefs are measured. Students learn to read opinion data carefully and connect opinion to democratic responsiveness.", "children": [{"id": "af10d91f-a934-446a-a528-c6725839540b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political Socialization and Political Identity", "children": [{"id": "48018b20-c12e-42d0-8cc1-4af4d7027c5c", "type": "concept", "title": "Family, schools, peers, religion, race, ethnicity, gender, class, region, generation, and life-cycle effects"}, {"id": "1e9dd963-54f2-46b4-a961-0a850ee1ed08", "type": "concept", "title": "Partisanship, ideology, political identity, group consciousness, political efficacy, trust in government"}, {"id": "34341657-c32b-4544-863a-71c480f00a5b", "type": "concept", "title": "Issue publics, low-information rationality, heuristics, motivated reasoning, affective polarization"}, {"id": "f15c0b23-e89e-4223-b12b-ff83cfeb5cc6", "type": "concept", "title": "Political culture, civic norms, patriotism, distrust, alienation, and democratic commitment"}], "description": "Students learn how people acquire political attitudes and identities over time."}, {"id": "26e46145-ca17-445f-a85e-fd3911cccfea", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Measuring Public Opinion", "children": [{"id": "21554323-06a0-406b-829d-79296f2c8376", "type": "concept", "title": "Population, sample, random sampling, sampling error, margin of error, confidence interval, weighting"}, {"id": "fb9f1dc0-0b15-463a-9274-d2f17007bf0a", "type": "concept", "title": "Question wording, question order, response options, social desirability bias, nonresponse bias, likely voter screens"}, {"id": "22f3aa3d-e811-441d-b648-9b8249a7af40", "type": "concept", "title": "Cross-tabulation, trend line, approval rating, favorability, issue salience, intensity, and panel survey"}, {"id": "106eac21-4979-4039-bbec-d13d5f730d3a", "type": "concept", "title": "Pew Research Center, Gallup, ANES, exit polls, polling averages, and survey transparency"}], "description": "Students learn how surveys work and why methods matter."}, {"id": "d416f76a-06b5-4397-bf17-bffd2e79ff8b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ideology, Polarization, and Democratic Responsiveness", "children": [{"id": "37c60615-6b63-4461-9834-aed7314b35b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Liberalism, conservatism, libertarianism, populism, socialism, nationalism, and multidimensional ideology"}, {"id": "a1a2fb59-2a01-4442-9cd2-1caa351e7014", "type": "concept", "title": "Elite polarization, mass polarization, affective polarization, sorting, negative partisanship, ideological consistency"}, {"id": "39b2d882-6f02-4444-82d9-fd82b1892095", "type": "concept", "title": "Representation, responsiveness, agenda setting, thermostatic opinion, policy mood, and public mandate claims"}, {"id": "35ff813d-53e0-4c5b-9804-53d537094505", "type": "concept", "title": "Polarization data, roll-call ideology, media fragmentation, geographic sorting, and institutional incentives"}], "description": "Students analyze the relationship between citizen preferences, elite behavior, parties, and policy."}]}, {"id": "16936e8f-7c58-4d11-8a00-1a09e2c5e3f2", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Participation, Voting Behavior, and Elections", "description": "Elections are central to representation, but participation is uneven and shaped by rules. Students learn how people participate, why they vote, and how electoral systems translate votes into power.", "children": [{"id": "11ea4673-ffba-404b-af36-a371390507d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Forms of Political Participation", "children": [{"id": "cb3320ce-076a-4bae-8f00-38fc76fbc980", "type": "concept", "title": "Voting, volunteering, donating, protesting, contacting officials, attending meetings, petitioning, boycotting, mutual aid"}, {"id": "4dc446a9-326c-43fb-b639-94b283523492", "type": "concept", "title": "Conventional participation, unconventional participation, digital activism, social movement participation, civic engagement"}, {"id": "b5ae1e9f-5e45-4f14-94f9-856ab4501dc4", "type": "concept", "title": "Political efficacy, mobilization, resources, recruitment, social networks, and participation gaps"}, {"id": "14697fd9-d748-48c4-bc14-fd203d347222", "type": "concept", "title": "Age, education, income, race, disability, language access, geography, and unequal voice"}], "description": "Students broaden participation beyond voting and evaluate different forms of civic action."}, {"id": "62edd281-ce3b-4c8f-877a-786c39608809", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Voting Behavior and Electoral Rules", "children": [{"id": "8c5f74b5-ad32-4038-bf6e-78bbb72f67fa", "type": "concept", "title": "Retrospective voting, prospective voting, party identification, candidate evaluation, issue voting, economic voting"}, {"id": "0d7445bd-c6dc-4e2a-9487-70ef5a771b16", "type": "concept", "title": "Registration, early voting, absentee voting, mail voting, voter ID, same-day registration, election administration"}, {"id": "d424a9a8-3071-4997-921a-ac8d56debf31", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary elections, caucuses, general elections, runoff elections, ranked-choice voting, plurality, majority, proportional representation"}, {"id": "93de236e-39f1-4f19-894f-ac2c103dbc92", "type": "concept", "title": "Turnout, undervote, overvote, ballot design, election security, certification, and election legitimacy"}], "description": "Students learn how individual choice and institutional design interact."}, {"id": "7f0135d2-4985-41f1-a2c0-d4d778242ae7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation and Electoral Geography", "children": [{"id": "0749baa9-18b7-417b-8a20-1b1669888e58", "type": "concept", "title": "Single-member districts, at-large elections, apportionment, redistricting, reapportionment, census"}, {"id": "b7f03caf-bc38-427b-bb5f-bcd21656d4c5", "type": "concept", "title": "Gerrymandering, packing, cracking, compactness, communities of interest, partisan symmetry, efficiency gap"}, {"id": "3a237bad-9429-4742-9d7c-2772bc1f14f6", "type": "concept", "title": "Electoral College, swing states, safe seats, battleground districts, national popular vote debate"}, {"id": "b2aa66ed-d641-4453-859d-63e947fd8086", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive representation, substantive representation, delegate, trustee, politico, and accountability"}], "description": "Students examine districts, apportionment, and gerrymandering as tools that shape representation."}]}, {"id": "460f586a-6c33-4331-9775-200799b4f4fd", "type": "topic", "title": "Political Parties, Campaigns, Interest Groups, and Lobbying", "description": "This section covers linkage institutions that organize political conflict. Students learn how parties, campaigns, and organized interests shape choices, agendas, and policy outcomes.", "children": [{"id": "a692a898-e400-4f32-bb3a-fbbfce97f17a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political Parties and Party Systems", "children": [{"id": "27994405-d205-48a1-b773-7cae8fdb915b", "type": "concept", "title": "Party organization, party in government, party in the electorate, party platform, party coalition"}, {"id": "1f07d9a8-644c-40bd-9945-5fb52034f7ae", "type": "concept", "title": "Two-party system, Duverger's law, realignment, dealignment, critical election, party sorting"}, {"id": "852c8eee-475d-47cb-8888-26a2dc99b8e8", "type": "concept", "title": "Democrats, Republicans, third parties, minor parties, independents, factions, and intra-party conflict"}, {"id": "7c147c26-6e2f-49b0-8397-8d3a8c083c42", "type": "concept", "title": "Party nominations, national committees, state parties, local parties, conventions, and party brands"}], "description": "Students learn why parties exist and how the American two-party system developed."}, {"id": "530127b6-bbb9-4a11-bd88-44b5063a1fb2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Campaigns, Money, and Electoral Strategy", "children": [{"id": "ff8fd8b4-b0e7-4688-bf6c-900e14f4ca06", "type": "concept", "title": "Candidate recruitment, campaign staff, field operation, voter targeting, canvassing, phone banking, digital ads"}, {"id": "cf55da1c-dc99-4b28-980e-64e3428f8ec3", "type": "concept", "title": "Hard money, soft money, PACs, Super PACs, independent expenditures, dark money, disclosure"}, {"id": "d5f69f2c-73dc-478d-a2bf-bc4ff4a3fe0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal Election Commission, campaign finance law, Buckley v. Valeo, Citizens United v. FEC"}, {"id": "53d65ec0-6c03-4108-b759-6182fc0957e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Polling, opposition research, debate strategy, fundraising, messaging, turnout operations, and campaign analytics"}], "description": "Students examine how campaigns persuade, mobilize, and allocate resources."}, {"id": "cc7dc622-1400-425d-9f88-af14980006d3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Interest Groups and Lobbying", "children": [{"id": "9fe05b6b-7dad-4db2-ae2b-03e9225d2eea", "type": "concept", "title": "Interest group, lobbyist, advocacy coalition, trade association, public interest group, social movement organization"}, {"id": "85db3568-7a10-44f0-865b-ef8adee2c8f8", "type": "concept", "title": "Inside lobbying, outside lobbying, grassroots lobbying, astroturfing, litigation, regulatory comments, campaign contributions"}, {"id": "32f604a5-ce2c-4dab-b81c-2275a30bb249", "type": "concept", "title": "Pluralism, elite theory, hyperpluralism, collective action, free rider problem, selective incentives"}, {"id": "ea3ed7d3-b7fc-44a8-912f-d78b44c077ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Iron triangles, issue networks, revolving door, capture, transparency, and ethics rules"}], "description": "Students learn how organized interests influence government and why lobbying raises representation and accountability questions."}]}, {"id": "48345a8c-902d-454c-bd7c-17b30c9581e0", "type": "topic", "title": "Media, Information, Misinformation, and Political Communication", "description": "Information environments shape what citizens know, what officials prioritize, and how accountability works. Students learn to evaluate media systems, campaign communication, and misinformation risks.", "children": [{"id": "c9f55ead-897c-486f-a91a-faa26f862d5a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Media Institutions and Agenda Setting", "children": [{"id": "018efe0c-b54d-4abf-a637-43ed1491c2fe", "type": "concept", "title": "Agenda setting, framing, priming, gatekeeping, watchdog role, horse-race coverage, investigative journalism"}, {"id": "43e6f92f-2e5f-4849-bc16-0f91738b87b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Public broadcasting, commercial media, local news, cable news, digital platforms, podcasts, newsletters"}, {"id": "8b13c25c-9a3b-48aa-9f51-80451c524e30", "type": "concept", "title": "Press freedom, libel, source protection, access journalism, leaks, press briefings, and public records"}, {"id": "276e507f-ca6c-4ef9-a07a-5631736875c1", "type": "concept", "title": "Media ownership, newsroom decline, news deserts, algorithmic distribution, and platform moderation"}], "description": "Students learn how news organizations and platforms influence what becomes politically visible."}, {"id": "68871638-617b-4d15-8168-94606df560a1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political Communication and Persuasion", "children": [{"id": "a962ad51-002d-42dd-a15e-022c15f07ff7", "type": "concept", "title": "Message framing, narrative, symbolism, issue ownership, wedge issues, dog whistles, campaign ads"}, {"id": "4b6cc98f-6073-4d54-8a5d-945124e9ce23", "type": "concept", "title": "Earned media, paid media, owned media, social media, microtargeting, influencer politics, and direct communication"}, {"id": "fcd179e7-1e1b-42bd-b294-564c8d926440", "type": "concept", "title": "Presidential rhetoric, State of the Union, bully pulpit, crisis communication, and executive messaging"}, {"id": "3c1f49cd-c18e-46c0-8a9a-7534f66b8f7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Emotional appeals, fear appeals, identity appeals, fact-checking, and persuasion limits"}], "description": "Students analyze how political actors craft messages and target audiences."}, {"id": "a2f0567c-7218-4460-b98a-02716a3806b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Misinformation, Disinformation, and Information Literacy", "children": [{"id": "46b0133d-2479-497a-a33f-b5612ff55f9b", "type": "concept", "title": "Misinformation, disinformation, malinformation, conspiracy theory, propaganda, bot, troll, deepfake"}, {"id": "0c09488c-d1be-4244-af39-2c61f1adea73", "type": "concept", "title": "Source evaluation, lateral reading, corroboration, evidence quality, context collapse, and reverse image search concept"}, {"id": "187a1d35-67f5-46a0-a798-101bf79d74b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Fact-checking, correction effects, backfire debate, pre-bunking, media literacy, and trust calibration"}, {"id": "01806c9e-0f7b-43af-a964-aeedef29da2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Election misinformation, health misinformation, foreign interference, platform governance, and democratic resilience"}], "description": "Students practice evaluating claims and recognizing information manipulation."}]}, {"id": "603b8c8a-85ab-470e-9f2b-e119e1804e0e", "type": "topic", "title": "Congress and the Legislative Process", "description": "Congress is the central lawmaking institution, but it is also a representative, deliberative, budgetary, and oversight body. Students learn how rules and incentives shape legislative action.", "children": [{"id": "0e8f416a-701f-4780-ac95-c36fe261b363", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Representation, Structure, and Leadership", "children": [{"id": "032b9cb2-399e-4f82-a3d9-7a106130fc9c", "type": "concept", "title": "House of Representatives, Senate, bicameralism, districts, states, apportionment, terms"}, {"id": "e3d9be81-6a5b-44b6-b837-efc8a036c835", "type": "concept", "title": "Speaker, majority leader, minority leader, whip, committee chair, ranking member, party caucus"}, {"id": "e4ec6446-7968-4db4-9326-919e907d5348", "type": "concept", "title": "Delegate model, trustee model, politico model, constituency service, casework, credit claiming, position taking"}, {"id": "7884c1ea-4efb-4499-b37f-395b7280fcff", "type": "concept", "title": "Incumbency advantage, home style, descriptive representation, substantive representation, and electoral connection"}], "description": "Students learn how Congress is organized and what members are trying to accomplish."}, {"id": "8b65954b-982b-4256-8f39-9b62026d1140", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Lawmaking, Committees, and Procedure", "children": [{"id": "b487acc2-e305-4fbc-9d50-4ad708871cd6", "type": "concept", "title": "Bill introduction, referral, committee hearing, markup, report, rules, floor debate, amendment, vote"}, {"id": "157e00fa-93b8-441a-b8ce-beb8d2b56aaa", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing committees, select committees, joint committees, conference committees, subcommittees"}, {"id": "d8e8e06d-0491-49c1-9eff-4996faf465f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Filibuster, cloture, holds, unanimous consent, reconciliation, discharge petition, veto override"}, {"id": "b2a46fc3-ca65-4582-960c-48a65a4975a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Logrolling, coalition-building, party discipline, agenda control, gridlock, and legislative entrepreneurship"}], "description": "Students trace how an idea becomes legislation and why many bills fail."}, {"id": "018ba8a5-c6ff-4b10-ac3b-f9caf5f1789f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Budgeting, Oversight, and Congressional Data", "children": [{"id": "6d0b26ea-0a27-4395-9dd0-e17f744055e1", "type": "concept", "title": "Authorization, appropriation, mandatory spending, discretionary spending, entitlement, deficit, debt ceiling"}, {"id": "0756faf0-efb3-44bc-aaca-df13959c18ca", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional Budget Office, Government Accountability Office, hearings, subpoenas, investigations, oversight letters"}, {"id": "e8702873-30a3-41b7-b58e-fce6aed9c996", "type": "concept", "title": "Roll-call votes, party unity scores, ideology scores, Voteview, committee reports, Congressional Record"}, {"id": "5d6b77eb-f523-4325-bdd4-ab21da49bc17", "type": "concept", "title": "Polarization, divided government, omnibus bills, continuing resolutions, shutdowns, and oversight politics"}], "description": "Students examine Congress as a budgetary and accountability institution."}]}, {"id": "d6accb3f-089d-4aac-9a9a-d7429218dd9e", "type": "topic", "title": "The Presidency and Executive Power", "description": "The presidency combines constitutional authority, administrative leadership, public persuasion, and crisis management. Students learn how presidential power has expanded and where it is constrained.", "children": [{"id": "6bbe3017-518b-428d-8f03-bf7f3ac85efc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Constitutional and Institutional Powers", "children": [{"id": "14d146ed-584b-43fd-b6da-e9768e1de196", "type": "concept", "title": "Commander in chief, chief executive, veto power, appointments, pardons, treaties, State of the Union"}, {"id": "19a8247e-c852-4b1c-8574-575afc7903b8", "type": "concept", "title": "Take Care Clause, executive orders, signing statements, executive agreements, proclamations, memoranda"}, {"id": "fc6a8573-33a0-4116-8e9d-93f8aee5c220", "type": "concept", "title": "Cabinet, Executive Office of the President, Office of Management and Budget, National Security Council"}, {"id": "7c2229db-bdc2-4e4d-83f4-06da274df816", "type": "concept", "title": "Appointments Clause, advice and consent, impeachment, Twenty-Second Amendment, Twenty-Fifth Amendment"}], "description": "Students learn the formal powers of the presidency and how they interact with other branches."}, {"id": "b4c58964-8616-4648-bcd5-77d3103e25c7", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Presidential Leadership and Public Politics", "children": [{"id": "373e207f-5f71-42c1-bdca-1bc7c8f310d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Bully pulpit, going public, approval rating, honeymoon period, mandate, national address, press conference"}, {"id": "1b7585d6-6667-4305-899f-f5aa4e708beb", "type": "concept", "title": "Agenda setting, coalition management, party leadership, public opinion strategy, and media management"}, {"id": "87ad81c0-7c14-4e9b-9333-d86ccff0b023", "type": "concept", "title": "Presidential character, leadership style, unilateral action, Neustadt's persuasion theory, and institutional presidency"}, {"id": "f43b2021-acf2-424a-8e3b-cb65150ce1a1", "type": "concept", "title": "Executive-legislative relations, divided government, polarization, veto bargaining, and crisis leadership"}], "description": "Students examine how presidents persuade, set agendas, and claim mandates."}, {"id": "9d236070-9cef-4314-aa82-4a8536b2db21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Foreign Affairs, Emergency Power, and Accountability", "children": [{"id": "dd6c124a-c8aa-42e5-b071-4db9d0e96e96", "type": "concept", "title": "War powers, foreign affairs power, commander-in-chief authority, treaty power, executive agreement"}, {"id": "9b9c8cbd-9b37-4060-ae73-64fad5207924", "type": "concept", "title": "War Powers Resolution, national emergency, sanctions, intelligence, covert action, and military authorization"}, {"id": "94c853d7-625e-456b-b43b-fea5bd711f43", "type": "concept", "title": "Youngstown framework, inherent power, statutory authorization, congressional oversight, and judicial review"}, {"id": "9f993235-5e0c-48c3-86cd-641d2669071d", "type": "concept", "title": "Impeachment, inspector general, media scrutiny, elections, congressional investigations, and constitutional norms"}], "description": "Students learn why executive power is often strongest in crises and foreign affairs."}]}, {"id": "24de1b0c-cb98-4aad-a640-be67445c165f", "type": "topic", "title": "The Federal Judiciary and Constitutional Review", "description": "Courts interpret law, resolve disputes, and shape constitutional meaning. Students learn court structure, judicial decision-making, and the political significance of judicial review.", "children": [{"id": "19e157f1-334a-4f71-871b-b03664150d57", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Court Structure and Jurisdiction", "children": [{"id": "dedce642-02c7-49a9-8e35-30cbe885e626", "type": "concept", "title": "District courts, courts of appeals, Supreme Court, jurisdiction, original jurisdiction, appellate jurisdiction"}, {"id": "17427274-6d05-48c4-b4c5-6a44d2205efd", "type": "concept", "title": "Federal question, diversity jurisdiction, writ of certiorari, docket, oral argument, opinion, dissent, concurrence"}, {"id": "c3ca1e9c-53dd-456c-b8be-f0ebfa2503d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Standing, ripeness, mootness, political question doctrine, advisory opinions, and justiciability"}, {"id": "3c2286b6-86f6-4c58-aded-197f380cba08", "type": "concept", "title": "Trial court, appellate review, precedent, remand, injunction, class action, and amicus curiae brief"}], "description": "Students learn how cases move through the federal judiciary."}, {"id": "16fd2a13-43d2-4b53-a997-67bd31492ad5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Judicial Review and Constitutional Decision-Making", "children": [{"id": "8f2f48d0-322c-4269-a0b8-9f98d04bf3ce", "type": "concept", "title": "Marbury v. Madison, judicial review, supremacy, constitutional avoidance, severability"}, {"id": "de94b15d-071b-4fa2-b838-30cabd882b2d", "type": "concept", "title": "Strict scrutiny, intermediate scrutiny, rational basis review, compelling interest, narrow tailoring"}, {"id": "9f9ec7b8-353a-479e-af4f-cec4e18683be", "type": "concept", "title": "Stare decisis, precedent, overruling, original meaning, textual analysis, doctrinal tests, balancing tests"}, {"id": "3d47c2b9-6bc4-4811-8cb4-0014afea39d8", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial restraint, judicial activism, legitimacy, countermajoritarian difficulty, and public confidence"}], "description": "Students learn how courts evaluate government action and create constitutional doctrine."}, {"id": "985aac45-41d9-426c-8650-808fd0ba2dfd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Supreme Court Politics and Landmark Cases", "children": [{"id": "786eaa36-4235-48ad-aa19-71593d6ecf9f", "type": "concept", "title": "Judicial appointments, senatorial courtesy, confirmation hearings, life tenure, judicial independence"}, {"id": "2962afb0-d7c8-49f7-82e3-3d816d4b458f", "type": "concept", "title": "Legal model, attitudinal model, strategic model, median justice, opinion assignment, cert pool"}, {"id": "5fc6bab8-c425-42c8-9ec4-13ca5fa1779d", "type": "concept", "title": "Landmark cases on federal power, rights, liberties, equality, elections, executive power, and administrative authority"}, {"id": "94af806f-7acd-4b1d-b4bd-f1079f738b45", "type": "concept", "title": "Oyez, Supreme Court opinions, SCOTUSblog, Constitution Annotated, and case brief method"}], "description": "Students analyze the Court as both a legal and political institution."}]}, {"id": "a0a58ff6-8a5b-4bab-9bbb-a67a9a780b01", "type": "topic", "title": "Bureaucracy, Public Administration, and Implementation", "description": "Most policy becomes real through administration. Students learn how agencies regulate, deliver services, exercise discretion, and balance expertise with democratic accountability.", "children": [{"id": "a47ff77f-1a39-46f8-b295-eac7433533b5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Administrative Agencies and Bureaucratic Organization", "children": [{"id": "a87c38ce-4f4e-424e-9001-7d4869944f18", "type": "concept", "title": "Cabinet departments, independent agencies, independent regulatory commissions, government corporations"}, {"id": "043efec0-6f63-40a7-9eda-f68843f4a1c9", "type": "concept", "title": "Bureaucracy, hierarchy, specialization, merit system, civil service, political appointees, street-level bureaucrats"}, {"id": "034b8857-1587-4dcb-8d3c-7421bf83628c", "type": "concept", "title": "Rulemaking, adjudication, enforcement, guidance, grants, service delivery, procurement, and licensing"}, {"id": "ce96449a-62b3-43db-9824-4f13c2272c6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Expertise, standard operating procedures, organizational culture, discretion, and administrative capacity"}], "description": "Students learn what bureaucracies are and why modern government depends on them."}, {"id": "410add14-1d90-4626-95c2-d46bc8ecbf75", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Implementation and Administrative Discretion", "children": [{"id": "71c6c7f9-2711-47a9-a48b-76ba02ea6216", "type": "concept", "title": "Implementation, policy design, target population, administrative burden, eligibility, take-up, compliance"}, {"id": "94931d3e-b01a-4923-8dc9-132031e9e4db", "type": "concept", "title": "Rulemaking process, notice and comment, Federal Register, Code of Federal Regulations, guidance documents"}, {"id": "fff99241-1fc3-48b0-84a9-c70917382027", "type": "concept", "title": "Street-level discretion, enforcement priorities, resource constraints, principal-agent problem, and performance management"}, {"id": "006cd502-cb37-42ab-82a9-5cf849dad1fb", "type": "concept", "title": "Unintended consequences, implementation failure, feedback effects, and policy learning"}], "description": "Students examine the gap between laws on paper and outcomes in practice."}, {"id": "fd5d1025-533f-4563-898d-a1c3da7298cd", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Accountability, Ethics, and Reform", "children": [{"id": "b3389805-118d-4cb4-a222-91b5e71650eb", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional oversight, presidential control, judicial review, inspectors general, audits, transparency"}, {"id": "3c3f97fb-0f64-437e-9d89-01e0fbcc5632", "type": "concept", "title": "Administrative Procedure Act, Freedom of Information Act, ethics rules, whistleblowers, conflicts of interest"}, {"id": "61e9b3f9-e258-4fd7-a825-6398527a37be", "type": "concept", "title": "Public value, public service motivation, equity, legitimacy, responsiveness, and professionalism"}, {"id": "b8f4e76b-2c3d-421b-900d-06af63bb10b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Red tape, regulatory capture, privatization, contracting, performance reform, and digital government"}], "description": "Students learn how bureaucracies are controlled and why accountability is complicated."}]}, {"id": "ebf1a4f5-279a-4792-bc6a-62371814fdc5", "type": "topic", "title": "Public Policy Process, Budgeting, and Policy Analysis", "description": "This section shows how institutions produce policy choices. Students practice defining problems, identifying stakeholders, comparing alternatives, and thinking about budgets and implementation.", "children": [{"id": "84aa30f5-fa73-4371-b94b-1e9ff4665356", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy Process and Agenda Setting", "children": [{"id": "891ae666-cf3a-457f-9a98-a6f968fc182c", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy cycle, problem definition, agenda setting, policy formulation, adoption, implementation, evaluation"}, {"id": "3b6f322c-4176-4872-911f-d5d62a516fb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Kingdon's multiple streams, problem stream, policy stream, politics stream, policy window, policy entrepreneur"}, {"id": "1ab8d614-924a-499e-a514-e2c9972e3157", "type": "concept", "title": "Stakeholders, institutions, incentives, interest groups, public opinion, media attention, and focusing events"}, {"id": "6e7dbac3-2b8a-4356-8e06-acc80e9fb4a5", "type": "concept", "title": "Framing, causal story, target populations, blame, credit, and political feasibility"}], "description": "Students learn how issues become public problems and reach decision-makers."}, {"id": "f4c6f4f6-70e5-41e7-af44-28bcbf9255b4", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy Analysis and Decision Criteria", "children": [{"id": "71adaffe-f033-4a44-b471-16fa71f18f22", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy alternatives, criteria, tradeoffs, cost, effectiveness, efficiency, equity, legality, feasibility, sustainability"}, {"id": "4362226f-6464-481f-9fa2-cdf77d789414", "type": "concept", "title": "Evidence, uncertainty, assumptions, sensitivity analysis, distributional effects, administrative capacity, and risk"}, {"id": "08cef9bf-232b-498f-b4c6-fcbf02144c50", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy memo, executive summary, recommendation, implementation plan, evaluation plan, and stakeholder analysis"}, {"id": "88525a04-7adf-4a6e-8f13-5fcd7d4af602", "type": "concept", "title": "Bardach's eightfold path, problem statement, options matrix, and transparent reasoning"}], "description": "Students learn a practical framework for comparing policy options."}, {"id": "c063a820-4a77-4ba4-bc24-767747d49ebc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Budgeting, Fiscal Policy, and Public Resources", "children": [{"id": "278f0d47-ca23-474e-8a6f-15fdd425d6f9", "type": "concept", "title": "Revenue, taxation, spending, appropriations, authorization, deficit, debt, mandatory spending, discretionary spending"}, {"id": "cf74bb68-8a08-4de8-8f7d-574f6c91b043", "type": "concept", "title": "Congressional Budget Office, Office of Management and Budget, fiscal year, baseline, cost estimate, scoring"}, {"id": "be2d8654-7730-4e18-a4de-4798b8b0f0bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Entitlements, grants, tax expenditures, public goods, externalities, redistribution, and fiscal federalism"}, {"id": "10dde901-a056-4686-9cad-e74c22b1a36c", "type": "concept", "title": "Budget tradeoffs, opportunity cost, accountability, transparency, and public value"}], "description": "Students learn how budgets express priorities and constrain choices."}]}, {"id": "9901bd25-a46c-4edf-9ffa-b5d39de3f94d", "type": "topic", "title": "American Political Economy, Inequality, and Domestic Policy", "description": "Domestic policy reveals what government does and whom it serves. Students connect institutions to markets, inequality, social provision, regulation, and everyday policy outcomes.", "children": [{"id": "76d14d16-b794-42df-8a5b-207450abdff0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Government, Markets, and Public Value", "children": [{"id": "61040022-119b-453f-9618-702bc8897848", "type": "concept", "title": "Market failure, public goods, externalities, monopoly, information asymmetry, collective action"}, {"id": "35d335cb-4170-4c71-ae6c-7d01ac1f39bf", "type": "concept", "title": "Regulation, taxation, spending, subsidies, social insurance, safety net, public investment, and redistribution"}, {"id": "4273f35d-2483-455f-8d91-1fb249da6b37", "type": "concept", "title": "Efficiency, equity, liberty, security, sustainability, feasibility, and public value"}, {"id": "fc993b17-fd13-4acb-b9d2-1c11d01a09e3", "type": "concept", "title": "Markets, governments, civil society, nonprofits, private contractors, and public-private partnerships"}], "description": "Students learn when and why government intervenes in economic and social life."}, {"id": "732bfcc8-d327-4b37-b80f-70ca3d393f90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Inequality and Social Policy", "children": [{"id": "9ca89896-0cab-42c8-9972-6edd35da957c", "type": "concept", "title": "Income inequality, wealth inequality, poverty, mobility, racial wealth gap, gender wage gap, geographic inequality"}, {"id": "114be223-b531-40be-b5b5-f830154ed610", "type": "concept", "title": "Social Security, Medicare, Medicaid, SNAP, TANF, unemployment insurance, housing assistance, tax credits"}, {"id": "ed3fdc5d-24c6-486c-b7c8-e78cf2887cbf", "type": "concept", "title": "Education policy, health policy, labor policy, family policy, criminal justice policy, and immigration policy"}, {"id": "32d1a579-53ed-4c9c-9486-f525be52b3f7", "type": "concept", "title": "Universal programs, means-tested programs, eligibility, stigma, administrative burden, and take-up"}], "description": "Students examine how policy shapes opportunity, security, and distribution."}, {"id": "afee8eae-e09b-43eb-9826-2b91aff4d024", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Policy Feedback and Political Conflict", "children": [{"id": "e4895ed6-46e9-4d7e-b2ec-35444961ac6c", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy feedback, path dependence, constituencies, lock-in, backlash, reform coalitions, and institutional veto points"}, {"id": "945e2925-71a1-4e11-82c8-63250652a0a6", "type": "concept", "title": "Winners and losers, concentrated benefits, diffuse costs, interest-group mobilization, and public opinion effects"}, {"id": "00f22af6-6dcc-46f5-b21d-75ccc56af264", "type": "concept", "title": "Federalism and policy variation, state capacity, local implementation, and inequality across places"}, {"id": "27d3926c-8e13-4c38-a3b9-69535529a012", "type": "concept", "title": "Case studies: health care, climate policy, voting rights, tax policy, policing, or education finance"}], "description": "Students learn how policies reshape politics after they are enacted."}]}, {"id": "dd293e5a-91fb-42b2-ae29-d936cbbcbf66", "type": "topic", "title": "Civic Skills, Primary Sources, Data Literacy, and Democratic Practice", "description": "The final section turns course knowledge into civic practice. Students learn to read government sources, interpret political data, evaluate claims, deliberate respectfully, and communicate policy arguments.", "children": [{"id": "1156ef0a-1e9d-4ef8-8814-19f4cd1fc05a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Primary-Source and Legal-Political Research Skills", "children": [{"id": "c0094b20-2d6e-43a5-83b5-8ac149bf7431", "type": "concept", "title": "Constitution, statutes, bills, committee reports, executive orders, agency rules, court opinions, briefs"}, {"id": "7727a194-aa24-4a70-b9d7-cde372a16453", "type": "concept", "title": "Congress.gov, Federal Register, Supreme Court website, Constitution Annotated, CRS reports, CBO reports"}, {"id": "1d70f1a5-258d-4b38-a026-91ad3bab6ad5", "type": "concept", "title": "Primary source, secondary source, citation, context, authorship, institutional role, legal authority, and date of publication"}, {"id": "4a626664-7559-4505-b90d-1ad95a9ba3d0", "type": "concept", "title": "Case briefing, bill tracking, committee research, agency rule review, and source credibility"}], "description": "Students practice finding and interpreting official government materials."}, {"id": "bc807a6a-20d2-4344-b0e6-373dbc01f362", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Political Data and Evidence Literacy", "children": [{"id": "020a89d3-5b17-4e94-a6b1-498de862fcdb", "type": "concept", "title": "Survey data, election returns, turnout data, roll-call votes, ideology scores, budget data, demographic data"}, {"id": "9f546ec7-83f2-44ce-9dc9-b1b23e0df200", "type": "concept", "title": "Descriptive statistics, trend, comparison, percentage point, rate, correlation, causation, outlier, and baseline"}, {"id": "0d49cb0b-b5d4-4a71-a038-4192c7cb2e45", "type": "concept", "title": "Pew, ANES, Census, Voteview, ICPSR, CBO, CRS, state election data, and open data portals"}, {"id": "66d129fc-3c24-4758-8496-79c684cbe9a9", "type": "concept", "title": "Graph reading, misleading axes, cherry-picking, ecological fallacy, sampling problems, and uncertainty"}], "description": "Students learn to evaluate political claims using data without needing advanced statistics."}, {"id": "0d78da40-0ad7-423e-af83-f8ac450d854f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Democratic Deliberation and Civic Action", "children": [{"id": "f207d08b-bcd4-4450-90fc-da40d9108f8d", "type": "concept", "title": "Deliberation, civil disagreement, active listening, argument mapping, evidence-based claims, values clarification"}, {"id": "968c07bb-6407-4eec-a2da-1613001cc49f", "type": "concept", "title": "Public comment, contacting representatives, attending meetings, coalition-building, volunteering, voting, advocacy"}, {"id": "5ee665e8-f6a0-4044-b2bf-bf8629760258", "type": "concept", "title": "Policy memo, testimony, op-ed, civic reflection, stakeholder briefing, and oral presentation"}, {"id": "7d80d129-2161-4895-b187-cd2a9e822851", "type": "concept", "title": "Democratic norms, pluralism, accountability, ethical advocacy, misinformation resistance, and lifelong civic learning"}], "description": "Students synthesize the course by practicing constructive engagement with disagreement and public problems."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$amgovjson$::jsonb,
